// expect-error: NITPICK-MACRO-005
// expect-error-at: 28:24
// expect-note: NITPICK-MACRO-009
// expect-note-at: 30:14
// expect-error: NITPICK-MACRO-005
// expect-error-at: 32:30
// expect-note: NITPICK-MACRO-009
// expect-note-at: 34:14
//
// A FIELD HAS NO INITIALISER, so a variable declaration carrying one cannot become
// a field. Refused rather than dropped: splicing this quietly would lose the value
// the author wrote, and losing it silently is worse than not accepting it.
//
// A QUALIFIER IS CARRIED, NOT REFUSED (1.0.8): `wild int8->:buf;` in a macro
// body splices as the `wild` field it is — a field has the same qualifier slot
// a declaration has, and until 1.0.8 the splice wrote zero into it and refused
// the declaration for "carrying" what the field "could not". Covered by the
// accept suite's splice cases, not here: this file is the refusal.
//
// AND THE QUALIFIER DOES NOT EXCUSE THE VALUE (1.6.1e step 3; the library
// listener's mc0085b, read as a documentation row): `fixed int32:b;` splices as a
// `fixed` field -- tests/analysis/rejection/spliced_fixed_field.npk pins that the
// qualifier arrived, by the write it refuses -- while `fixed int32:b = 5i32;` is
// still this refusal. The reference's sentence said a qualifier was "refused
// rather than stripped"; it is neither refused nor stripped, it travels.
mod:field_initialiser;

macro:with_init = () { int32:a = 5i32; };

struct:S = { #with_init(); };

macro:fixed_with_init = () { fixed int32:b = 5i32; };

struct:T = { #fixed_with_init(); };

func:main = int32(cstring[]:_~argv) { exit 0i32; };
